import marks "sngl:internal/marks"

// A top-level window — a surface the user sees, which a program creates and
// destroys with the tree. `title` names it; `favicon` is its icon. `@error` is
// the window's outermost error boundary. A window is not a destination: the
// pages a user goes between are sngl:ui/nav's `page`s, held by a `stack` the
// window shows, and the window around the stack is the chrome every page
// shares -- `title="Docs - {pages.current.title}"`.
//
// The #[builtin("window")] mark is how the checker recognises a window
// declaration — by tag, not by matching the name "window".
//
// A program must declare at least one: the package body renders nothing on
// its own, so a program with no window has nowhere to draw.
//
// `visible` is whether the window is on screen, on a target whose windows the
// program can show and hide: gtk4 and fyne, and bubbletea and android, which
// show one window and draw nothing while it is hidden. On html the first
// window that is always there is the document, which cannot leave the screen
// and is told of no close, so its `visible` and `@closed` do nothing; every
// other window is a `<dialog>` in it, shown and hidden as a desktop window is.
// It is a two-way prop: a window manager's close is the host reporting
// `visible = false`, the way a checkbox reports a click, and `@closed` fires
// after it. On bubbletea the close is ctrl+c, and on android the system back. So `:visible=shown` writes
// `shown` back, and a window nothing binds keeps the value in a cell of its
// own, which `open` and `close` below write. What the tree says decides what
// exists: a window under `if details` whose `@closed` writes
// `details = false` is destroyed, widgets and effects with it, and hiding one
// without destroying it is `visible`. A close that leaves no window on screen
// ends the program, unless it started with none.
#[marks.builtin("window")]
component window(
    title string,
    favicon string,
    :visible bool = true,
    @error error,
    @closed(),
    content ...component node,
) root {
    // Puts the window on screen: `visible = true`, which is no change to a
    // window already there, so it does not raise one. Reached
    // through the window's `#id` -- `window #details(…)`, then
    // `details.open()` from a handler -- which is what lets a program started
    // with no window on screen, one whose `@run` never called `run`, bring
    // one up in answer to something. The write lands on whatever `visible`
    // is: the caller's var where `:visible=shown` bound one, the window's own
    // cell where nothing did -- on html's document too, which stays on
    // screen whatever its `visible` says.
    func open() {
        visible = true
    }

    // Takes the window off screen without ending the program:
    // `visible = false`. `open` brings it back as it was.
    func close() {
        visible = false
    }
}
